WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.809
Finance wants each merchant's volume for March.

2
00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:09.049
Here are March's ten payments, at four merchants.

3
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:13.277
You could send every row to Kotlin and add them up there.

4
00:00:13.277 --> 00:00:18.527
On the large data set, that ships a million rows to compute 2,500 numbers.

5
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:21.672
Let the database add them up where the data lives.

6
00:00:21.672 --> 00:00:26.725
First, WHERE looks at one row at a time, and drops the failed and the pending payment.

7
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.378
GROUP BY merchant sorts the rest into piles, one for each merchant.

8
00:00:31.378 --> 00:00:33.249
Eight rows make four groups.

9
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.675
Then each aggregate folds a pile into one value.

10
00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:39.647
Count counts its rows, and sum adds them up.

11
00:00:39.647 --> 00:00:41.944
Four groups give four output rows.

12
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.331
Risk only wants merchants whose sum passes 10,000.

13
00:00:45.331 --> 00:00:48.498
Put the sum in the WHERE, and PostgreSQL refuses.

14
00:00:48.498 --> 00:00:52.957
WHERE runs before any group exists, so there's nothing to add up yet.

15
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.518
HAVING is the same kind of filter, one step later.

16
00:00:56.518 --> 00:01:00.345
It tests the folded rows, so merchants 1 and 7 drop out.

17
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.989
So WHERE decides which rows go into the piles, and HAVING decides which piles come out.

18
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:09.791
Finance also wants a total per country, and a grand total.

19
00:01:09.791 --> 00:01:14.415
Three GROUP BY queries glued together would read the payments three times.

20
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.062
ROLLUP of country and merchant lists three groupings in one GROUP BY.

21
00:01:19.062 --> 00:01:24.171
The first is country and merchant: the same four groups, now sorted by country.

22
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.367
The second set is country alone.

23
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:31.643
Each country's groups fold into one subtotal row, with NULL where the merchant was.

24
00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:35.448
The last set is empty: one group that holds every row.

25
00:01:35.448 --> 00:01:36.988
That's the grand total.

26
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:43.189
Eight rows in all: four merchants, three subtotals and one grand total, from one query.

27
00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:47.540
Sort the rows into groups by key, and fold each group into one row.

28
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:52.212
WHERE filters rows before the grouping, and HAVING filters groups after it.
